  "science": [
    {
      "claim": "Mars Climate Orbiter was lost on 23 September 1999 as it was to enter orbit around Mars, after a ~9.5 month cruise. Mission cost: spacecraft about US$125 million.",
      "source": "NASA, Mars Climate Orbiter Mishap Investigation Board, Phase I Report, 10 Nov 1999."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Root cause: ground software supplied thruster impulse data in English units (pound-force seconds) where the navigation software expected metric units (newton-seconds); 1 lbf = 4.45 N, so the real impulse was 4.45 times what navigators assumed.",
      "source": "NASA MIB Phase I Report, 1999."
    },
    {
      "claim": "TO VERIFY BEFORE PUBLISHING: approach altitude about 57 km against a minimum survivable altitude of about 80 km; spacecraft cost about US$125 million. Figures from public summaries; the report PDF could not be fetched from this session. The planned altitude (the brief said 150 km; the report may say about 226 km) is NOT used in the film.",
      "source": "NASA MIB Phase I Report; NASA JPL mission pages."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Simplification: \"Un software ... otro lo lee\" compresses the contractor ground software (SM_FORCES) and the NASA navigation team. The probe is shown as vector art; what happened to it after the pass is not known (probably burned up or skipped off), the video only says the signal was lost."
    }
  ],
